d yet, keep the draft, and offer retry.\",\n          \"why\": \"A failed request must not produce a false success message.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"An error clears every field and says Something went wrong.\",\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI to preserve input, identify the correction and manage focus to the error.\",\n      \"after\": \"A person can recover without re-entering the appointment.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Watch a person attempt the task without coaching. If unavailable, label your own walkthrough and seek outside feedback later.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"A confirmation journey people can finish\",\n      \"brief\": \"Create a prototype for finding an appointment and confirming access, including errors and narrow-screen keyboard use.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Draw the visitor's goal, the steps they take and the possible outcomes in plain language.\",\n        \"Ask AI to make a small working preview. Include a successful path and useful responses to missing information or a failed connection.\",\n        \"With permission, watch someone try it, or label your own trial as a practice scenario. Improve one confusing part and repeat the task.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The normal journey reaches a clearly identified result.\",\n        \"Missing input and a simulated connection failure preserve useful work and give a next action.\",\n        \"Narrow-screen and keyboard observations are recorded, together with the source of user feedback.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain why error recovery is part of the journey.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Show the label, focus order and error message for one input.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What would a user believe if a failed request displayed Confirmed?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare a prototype with a working connected service. What does each establish?\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says its accessibility score proves every user can finish the task. What observations are still needed?\",\n      \"guide\": \"Ask the learner to complete the task by keyboard and explain a fresh error state before trying it.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The person uses a screen reader and cannot see a colour change. Improve the feedback.\",\n        \"Long translated labels wrap on a small phone. Revise and check the journey.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [13, 26]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 9,\n    \"stage\": 2,\n    \"title\": \"Compare proposed approaches\",\n    \"problem\": \"The owner already pays for a scheduling product. A custom app is only one option. Compare buying, configuring, integrating and building against the actual workflow.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A documented choice with costs, boundaries and a realistic alternative.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"product\", \"architecture\", \"stack\", \"evidence\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Buy, configure, integrate or build\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Buying adopts a product, configuration adjusts supported behaviour, integration connects existing systems, and building creates new behaviour. Combinations are common. Compare task fit, access, export, maintenance and total cost rather than novelty.\",\n        \"example\": \"Configure an access-confirmation field before building a second scheduling system.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Custom software is automatically cheaper because AI writes the code.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Boundaries and total cost\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A boundary separates responsibilities. Every connection introduces a contract and a dependency. Total cost includes setup, usage, maintenance, migration and people's time. A low entry price can hide an expensive constraint.\",\n        \"example\": \"A free plan may lack the export or permissions the workflow needs.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A provider's current