uration option. Choose a small reversible trial.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"List what your project must do, who controls its data and what access is available in your setting.\",\n        \"Ask AI to compare current options. Check important claims against their sources and estimate both setup cost and ongoing cost over the same period.\",\n        \"Record your choice, one alternative and a drawing of which parts connect. Explain what new information would make you choose differently.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"Both approaches are compared using the same workflow and cost criteria.\",\n        \"Important capability and pricing claims have dated sources or explicit unknowns.\",\n        \"The selected trial has an exit/export plan and a reason to revisit the choice.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain your approach and who owns each responsibility.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find an integration boundary or the reason no integration is needed.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What happens if the provider removes a necessary export?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Defend the chosen approach against the strongest alternative, including maintenance.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says free code generation makes ongoing cost zero. Name the missing costs and how to estimate them.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Introduce a changed budget or access constraint; check that the learner can revise the choice without defending a tool by habit.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The current provider doubles its price. Recalculate the comparison.\",\n        \"The team needs offline operation and a local language. Reassess suitability.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [10, 26, 28]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 10,\n    \"stage\": 2,\n    \"title\": \"Specify what counts as done\",\n    \"problem\": \"The confirmation prototype looks finished. Before connecting real data, define the observable results so tests do not merely repeat whatever the implementation happens to do.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A small acceptance matrix covering normal, boundary and failure cases.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"quality\", \"logic\", \"product\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Acceptance criteria and examples\",\n        \"explanation\": \"An acceptance criterion names a required behaviour. A concrete example gives a starting state, action and expected observation. Write the expectation before implementation so it can reveal the wrong behaviour.\",\n        \"example\": \"Given an unconfirmed visit, saving confirmed access changes only that visit and displays its identifier.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A test passes, therefore the requirement must be correct.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Boundaries and negative cases\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Boundary cases sit at the edge of a rule. Negative cases try an action that should be refused or fail. Include what must remain unchanged after failure, not just which error appears.\",\n        \"example\": \"Submitting an empty appointment ID must not update any record.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"One successful example covers every meaningful situation.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Requirement\",\n        \"runs\": \"Agreed brief\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Given / action / expected\",\n        \"runs\": \"Acceptance matrix\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Observed evidence\",\n        \"runs\": \"Actual product\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Choose the evidence proposed for 'only the chosen visit changes'.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Success toast\",\n          \"result\": \"Insufficient: a message does not establish which records changed.\",\n          \"why\": \"Check the saved record and another record that must remain unchanged.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Before/after records\",\n          \"result\": \"Relevant evidence if identity and environment are correct.\",\n          \"why\": \"The observation directly concerns the required state change.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"AI says all good\",\n          \"result\": \"A claim awaiting reproducible evidence.\",\n          \"why\": \"Ask what was run, on which data, and what was observed.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"Done means the Confirm button works.\",\n      \"action\": \"Specify valid, missing-ID and duplicate-submit cases with expected stored state.\",\n      \"after\": \"The checks can expose a success message attached to the wrong record.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"A useful check can fail for a real defect. A suite unable to fail may be testing a convenient story.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"The definition-of-done matrix\",\n      \"brief\": \"Write and trial acceptance cases for the planned stored confirmation workflow before implementing it further.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Choose three important actions from your brief. Make a table with the starting state, the action and the result you expect.\",\n        \"Include ordinary input, values at the allowed limits and failures. State what information should stay unchanged in each case.\",\n        \"Ask AI to find missing cases, then try the checks against your working preview. Mark checks of an unavailable server or other unfinished part as not yet run.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The matrix distinguishes expected results from observations and unrun checks.\",\n        \"It includes a normal, boundary and failure case with specific record identities.\",\n        \"At least one deliberate prototype defect or missing behaviour is identified by a check.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain why expectations should come from requirements.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Show one check that concerns state and one that concerns the interface.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What would your matrix show if the wrong visit were updated?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare a screen assertion with checking stored state; what can each miss?\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says 100% test coverage proves there are no defects. Explain the limit and propose a missing scenario.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Supply a defect that preserves the toast but changes the wrong record. Ask which evidence would expose it.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"A confirmation arrives twice. Add the expected behaviour.\",\n        \"The write fails after the form says Done. Revise the success condition.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [12, 22]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 11,\n    \"stage\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"Follow an action through the system\",\n    \"problem\": \"A staff member presses Confirm but the stored appointment does not change. Follow the action through the browser, network, server and response instead of guessing which screen to redesign.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A traceable request journey and one diagnosed failed response.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"networks\", \"execution\", \"stack\", \"architecture\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Client, server and HTTP contract\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A client requests work from a server. HTTP carries a request method, destination, headers and sometimes a body; the response has a status and data. DNS resolves a name to a destination. TLS protects transport when correctly used; it does not decide who is allowed to edit a visit.\",\n        \"example\": \"A browser sends a visit identifier and new status to an API endpoint.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"An HTTPS address means every action is authorized.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Asynchronous work and failure\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Network work takes time and can fail independently of the page. The interface