0 invalid request\",\n          \"result\": \"Show a correctable error; do not claim the visit changed.\",\n          \"why\": \"The server rejected this request contract.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Timeout\",\n          \"result\": \"Outcome is unknown. Check state before deciding how to retry.\",\n          \"why\": \"The write and the reply are separate events.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"The UI says Confirmed immediately when clicked.\",\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI to trace the actual request, handle pending and error states, and correlate the response to the visit.\",\n      \"after\": \"The interface reports the server's observed result instead of assuming success.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Use the real network observation or logs. A prepared response is a teaching fixture, not a request to a deployed service.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"A connected confirmation journey\",\n      \"brief\": \"Use AI to implement or connect a small confirmation API for fictional visits. Trace one actual action and one failed request through the real stack.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Give AI your expected-result table. Ask it to connect the browser and server, define the messages they exchange, and provide instructions to run both parts.\",\n        \"With AI's help, inspect one actual network request. Identify its action, address, sent data and response code, and explain which parts run in the browser and on the server.\",\n        \"Try invalid input and a service that is unavailable. Save the feedback you observed and say when the final outcome is still unknown.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"A real valid action reaches the intended handler and returns the correct visit identity.\",\n        \"An invalid request produces the specified status and useful feedback.\",\n        \"The diagram accurately separates browser, application server and any external service; a failed call is recorded.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain the request and response, DNS, TLS and asynchronous waiting in this journey.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Identify the actual endpoint, runtime, dependencies and relevant configuration.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What if the server changes state but the reply is lost?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare a local browser calculation with a server request for shared data.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says a timeout proves the appointment is unchanged. What observation would resolve this?\",\n      \"guide\": \"Ask the learner to trace a fresh action and distinguish a rejected request from an unknown outcome.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The API returns a different visit's ID. Decide how the client should respond.\",\n        \"The server is reachable by address but its hostname fails. Locate a plausible boundary.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [9, 10, 24]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 12,\n    \"stage\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"Keep meaningful information\",\n    \"problem\": \"The appointment vanishes after the server restarts. Staff also have two people with the same name. Introduce persistent records, stable identifiers and information provenance.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A stored workflow with an accurate data model and source-quality notes.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"data\", \"evidence\", \"execution\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Persistence, identifiers and relationships\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Memory belongs to a running process; persistent storage survives the intended restart when configured correctly. A stable identifier distinguishes records even when names match. Relationships connect records, such as one customer with several visits. Constraints protect rules at the storage boundary.\",\n        \"example\": \"Visit V12 refers to customer C7 rather than identifying a person only by name.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"An in-memory database is automatically durable.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Queries, SQL and provenance\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A query asks for selected data; SQL is a language for relational data operations. A model defines fields and relationships. Record where values came from and what is missing: an empty access field differs from access denied.\",\n        \"example\": \"Query pending visits while keeping unknown access status separate from refused access.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Importing a spreadsheet makes all its contents trustworthy.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Validated visit\",\n        \"runs\": \"Application server\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Records + relationships\",\n        \"runs\": \"Configured persistent store\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Query after restart\",\n        \"runs\": \"Server → browser\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Choose the storage and data situation in this prepared model.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Process memory + restart\",\n          \"result\": \"The temporary visit disappears.\",\n          \"why\": \"No persistent write was made.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Persistent store + restart\",\n          \"result\": \"The saved visit can be queried after restarting with the same store.\",\n          \"why\": \"Persistence depends on the actual storage path, volume or managed service.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Two identical names\",\n          \"result\": \"Names are ambiguous; stable customer IDs preserve the correct relationship.\",\n          \"why\": \"A label is not necessarily a unique identity.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"Visits store only a customer's name and an empty access string.\",\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI for stable identifiers, a customer relationship and explicit unknown access status; retain the source of imported information.\",\n      \"after\": \"The correct customer's visit survives a restart and unknown data remains visible.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Inspect the actual storage configuration and perform the restart test. A database-shaped object in a demo is not proof of durability.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"An appointment register that survives\",\n      \"brief\": \"Persist fictional customers and visits with stable IDs, a relationship and explicit missing-data handling. Record where imported or entered information came from.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Ask AI what information each appointment needs, which values must be unique or valid, how records can be looked up, and where they should be stored.\",\n        \"Save two fictional customers with the same name. Give them different identifiers and attach each visit to the correct customer.\",\n        \"Restart using the project's instructions and look up the records again. Investigate any missing information or a value whose source you cannot support.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"Saved visits remain after the intended process restart using the same storage configuration.\",\n        \"Same-name customers remain distinguishable and visits connect to the correct identity.\",\n        \"Missing information has an explicit meaning and an important value has recorded provenance.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain memory, persistence, model, identifier, relationship and query.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find the actual data store, its configuration and one query with AI's help.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What happens if a deployment uses a temporary disk or a different database URL?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare one flat record list with related customer and visit records.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says every blank access value means refused access. What source evidence and data change are needed?\",\n      \"guide\": \"Ask the learner to identify a real saved record after restart and explain an unfamiliar missing-data case.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"A customer's displayed name changes. Explain which identifiers should remain stable.\",\n        \"An import has no source date. Explain what claims you cannot make.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [5, 6, 24]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 13,\n    \"stage\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"Protect correct state\",\n    \"problem\": \"Two staff members reserve the last appointment slot. A slow connection also makes one person press Save twice. Correct state must survive both situations.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"Validated updates with a demonstrated duplicate and concurrency policy.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"data\", \"logic\", \"architecture\", \"quality\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Validation and transactions\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Validation checks a value against rules. A transaction groups related storage changes so they commit together or roll back together. Checking availability and later writing without a suitable atomic rule can let two requests claim the same slot.\",\n        \"example\": \"Reserve the slot and create its visit together, or leave both unchanged.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A browser check prevents conflicting writes from other clients.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Concurrency and idempotency\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Concurrent operations overlap. Idempotency makes repeating the same intended operation avoid repeating its effect. A request key can identify a retry; it needs a policy for different data under the same key. Idempotency is not permission to skip validation or authorization.\",\n        \"example\": \"Retrying confirmation K17 returns its original result rather than creating a second visit.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Disabling the Save button is sufficient duplicate protection.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Validate request + identity\",\n        \"runs\": \"Server boundary\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Atomic storage rule\",\n        \"runs\": \"Database transaction / constraint\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Stable retry result\",\n        \"runs\": \"Server response\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Two prepared requests compete for one available slot.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Separate check then write\",\n          \"result\": \"Both may observe availability and create conflicting reservations.\",\n          \"why\": \"The gap between checking and writing permits a race.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Atomic reservation rule\",\n          \"result\": \"One reservation succeeds; the conflicting request gets a clear refusal.\",\n          \"why\": \"A suitable transaction or constraint protects the shared invariant.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Retry same request key\",\n          \"result\": \"Return the original result without a second reservation.\",\n          \"why\": \"A valid retry should not multiply the business effect.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"Two requests both see one free slot and both save.\",\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI to enforce the invariant at the storage boundary and supply a concurrent-request check.\",\n      \"after\": \"One wins; the other receives a conflict. Repeating the winner's request preserves one reservation.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"A sequential happy-path check cannot establish concurrency behaviour.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"One slot, one reservation\",\n      \"brief\": \"Protect the stored appointment workflow against invalid requests, repeated submissions and competing reservations.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Write a rule that must always hold, such as 'accepted bookings never exceed available slots'. Decide whether a repeated request should create a new booking.\",\n        \"Ask AI to check and reserve a slot as one operation, called an atomic change. Give each intended booking an identifier so repeated attempts can reuse it.\",\n        \"Try invalid input, the same request twice and two requests at once. Check the final stored bookings as well as the responses.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"An invalid request leaves the required stored state unchanged.\",\n        \"Repeating the same request produces one business effect.\",\n        \"Overlapping requests cannot both reserve the same exclusive slot; actual observations are recorded.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain the invariant, transaction, race and retry policy.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find where the shared-state rule is enforced in the actual system.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What if two valid requests arrive before either finishes?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare UI disabling, server validation and a storage constraint.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says one successful reservation test proves concurrent safety. Design evidence that could expose a race.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Introduce a reused request key with changed data. Ask the learner to explain an appropriate response.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"A retry uses the same key but a different slot. Define the response.\",\n        \"The second half of a two-part write fails. Explain required stored state.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [12, 24]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 14,\n    \"stage\": 3,\n    \"title\": \"Give people the right access\",\n    \"problem\": \"Customers may see their own visits; dispatchers may schedule assigned work. Hiding a button does not stop someone calling an endpoint directly.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A two-user permission demonstration with server-enforced boundaries.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"security\", \"data\", \"networks\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Authentication and sessions\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Authentication establishes an identity. A session lets later requests refer to an authenticated interaction through an appropriate credential. Credentials and session configuration need protection. Use a well-supported identity mechanism rather than inventing a password system for practice.\",\n        \"example\": \"The server associates a request with customer C7 using its validated session.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A customer ID supplied by the browser proves that person's identity.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Authorization and trust boundaries\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Authorization decides whether an identity may perform this action on this record. Enforce it at the server or trusted service boundary for every relevant request. Least privilege grants only the needed authority. Validation and transport encryption solve different problems.\",\n        \"example\": \"C7 cannot retrieve C8's private appointment by changing the URL.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A hidden admin button is an access-control rule.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Authenticated request\",\n        \"runs\": \"Client + identity service\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Permission on this record\",\n        \"runs\": \"Trusted server boundary\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Allowed result or refusal\",\n        \"runs\": \"API response\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Choose the prepared identity and record requested.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"C7 requests C7 visit\",\n          \"result\": \"Allowed when the session and action are valid.\",\n          \"why\": \"Identity and ownership match the policy.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"C7 requests C8 visit\",\n          \"result\": \"Refused without disclosing private visit details.\",\n          \"why\": \"An authenticated person is not authorized for every record.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"No valid session\",\n          \"result\": \"No private record returned; require appropriate sign-in.\",\n          \"why\": \"A supplied record identifier does not establish identity.\",\n          \"node\": 0\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"The API accepts any customerId sent by the page.\",\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI to derive identity from the trusted session and check ownership before returning a visit.\",\n      \"after\": \"Separate customers receive their own records and cross-customer access is denied.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Use separate identities in actual checks. Do not infer enforcement from what one screen shows.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"The right visit for the right person\",\n      \"brief\": \"Add a supported identity mechanism and an explicit permission policy to fictional visit data. Avoid real personal data in the learning project.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Write which actions customers and staff may take and whose records each person may read or change.\",\n        \"Ask AI to identify signed-in users and keep their sessions correctly. Enforce permission checks where records are read or changed, rather than relying only on hidden buttons.\",\n        \"Try two separate fictional users and a request with nobody signed in. Directly test actions that should be allowed and actions that should be refused.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"Each test user can perform the intended action on the permitted records.\",\n        \"A direct cross-user request cannot read or modify another user's private record.\",\n        \"An unauthenticated request is refused and useful