dled into public browser JavaScript.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Dependencies and safe failure\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A dependency can be unavailable or return an ambiguous result. Use timeouts, explicit status and suitable retry rules. External actions may need idempotency or reconciliation; repeating a message request can otherwise contact someone twice.\",\n        \"example\": \"A failed provider call leaves the reminder pending, not falsely delivered.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A simulated provider response proves a real message was delivered.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Authorized reminder request\",\n        \"runs\": \"Application server\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Configured provider adapter\",\n        \"runs\": \"Server → external service\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Status + reconciliation\",\n        \"runs\": \"Application records\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Change the prepared provider response. No messages are sent here.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Accepted by provider\",\n          \"result\": \"Record provider acceptance, not necessarily delivery to the person.\",\n          \"why\": \"Accepted and delivered are different contract states.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Provider unavailable\",\n          \"result\": \"Keep an explicit pending/failed state and an appropriate recovery path.\",\n          \"why\": \"Do not invent successful delivery.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Missing credential\",\n          \"result\": \"Fail configuration validation without exposing the secret.\",\n          \"why\": \"Credentials belong at the trusted integration boundary.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"The UI says Reminder delivered whenever the request is submitted.\",\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI to model accepted, delivered, failed and unknown states according to the provider contract.\",\n      \"after\": \"The record reflects observed provider evidence and a bounded retry policy.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Use a test inbox or authorized recipient. A local double checks your boundary, not external deliverability.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"A reminder integration with honest status\",\n      \"brief\": \"Connect a test messaging service or a clearly labelled local provider double. Keep credentials server-side and demonstrate unavailable-provider behaviour. Record which external claims remain untested.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"With AI, check what the reminder provider expects, how its test mode works, what it costs and which actions are allowed.\",\n        \"Ask AI to build a small connection to the provider. Check required settings, limit how long it waits, and recognise retries of the same intended reminder.\",\n        \"Try accepted, rejected and unavailable responses. Clearly distinguish a local simulated provider from an actual delivery in the provider's test mode.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The configuration and secret boundary are documented and secrets are absent from public output.\",\n        \"A successful boundary check records the contract's actual status without exaggerating delivery.\",\n        \"Provider failure produces an honest state and bounded recovery; no unintended contact occurs.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain the provider contract, dependency and secret boundary.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Identify actual adapter configuration and whether tests use a double or real service.\",\n      \"predict\": \"If a reply is lost after acceptance, what might a blind retry do?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare a provider double, test mode and an authorized real delivery.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says a passing mock test proves the customer received a message. Explain the gap.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Ask for the exact authority, environment and evidence behind the claimed external effect.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The provider changes its response schema. Explain the impact and investigation.\",\n        \"The recipient withdraws permission for reminders. Adapt the workflow.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [9, 10, 17, 23]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 16,\n    \"stage\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Give an agent a complete task\",\n    \"problem\": \"You want AI to add a useful change, not merely write a function. Give it the goal, context, authority and evidence requirements so it can plan and execute appropriately.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A completed delegated change with an inspectable decision and evidence record.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"agents\", \"collaboration\", \"quality\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Goals, context and decomposition\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A goal describes the accepted outcome. Context supplies the current system, users, constraints and relevant evidence. Decomposition breaks work into dependencies. AI may propose the plan and revise it; the learner must understand why the parts serve the outcome.\",\n        \"example\": \"Add an access-confirmation filter while preserving existing permissions and saved data.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A longer prompt or more prescribed steps always gives better delegation.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Authority, budgets and stopping\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Authority defines permitted actions and resources. A budget limits spend, time or work. A stopping condition describes completion or a reason to pause and report. Match controls to consequences and allow routine decisions within the agreed boundary.\",\n        \"example\": \"Work in a test environment; stop if credentials or a paid provider are required.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"An intelligent agent should automatically have unlimited access.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Goal + context + authority\",\n        \"runs\": \"Task brief\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Plan / act / observe\",\n        \"runs\": \"AI workspace + permitted tools\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Accepted outcome + evidence\",\n        \"runs\": \"Actual product review\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Choose the authority supplied to a prepared agent task.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"No boundary stated\",\n          \"result\": \"The task leaves spending, publication and data access ambiguous.\",\n          \"why\": \"Capability does not establish authorization.\",\n          \"node\": 0\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Test environment only\",\n          \"result\": \"The agent can work and test locally; external release needs a separate authorized step.\",\n          \"why\": \"A useful boundary permits meaningful work while clarifying consequences.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Bounded live change\",\n          \"result\": \"Use the named environment, budget and release/recovery conditions.\",\n          \"why\": \"Broader autonomy can be appropriate when its scope is explicit.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"Make the app smarter. Do whatever is needed.\",\n      \"action\": \"State the workflow outcome, actual stack, permitted resources, preserved behaviours and observable checks.\",\n      \"after\": \"The agent can choose implementation details and report a result against the brief.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Planning and review can also be delegated. Still connect their claims to the right objective and observations.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"Delegate a complete improvement\",\n      \"brief\": \"Give a capable AI a meaningful change to the visit application. Let it plan, implement, test and diagnose within explicit boundaries.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Choose one change. Give AI the current system, desired result, limits, allowed actions, budget and conditions that mean it must stop.\",\n        \"Let AI propose a plan and carry out the work it is allowed to do. Record important decisions and questions it needed answered.\",\n        \"Inspect the actual result and repeat a relevant user task. Update your explanation of how the changed system works.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The delegated task specifies outcome, context, authority, budget and stopping conditions.\",\n        \"The change meets prewritten acceptance criteria with identifiable observations.\",\n        \"The record distinguishes agent actions, learner decisions, unrun checks and remaining gaps.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain how this task permits autonomy without leaving authority ambiguous.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find the evidence connecting the agent's changes to the requested outcome.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What should happen if the plan needs access outside its allowed scope?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare prescribing every edit with delegating an accepted outcome.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says its own successful summary is sufficient acceptance evidence. How would you investigate?\",\n      \"guide\": \"Ask the learner to change a budget or authority limit and explain the effect on the plan and checks.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The selected tool requires a paid account outside budget. Revise the plan.\",\n        \"The agent discovers a useful change outside scope. Decide how to handle it.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [16, 20, 28]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 17,\n    \"stage\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Coordinate dependent work\",\n    \"problem\": \"A UI change and an API change can be worked on separately, but incompatible assumptions will break the whole journey. More agents also create coordination cost.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A coordinated change with a clear interface contract and integration evidence.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"agents\", \"architecture\", \"collaboration\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Interfaces, ownership and dependencies\",\n        \"explanation\": \"An interface is an agreement between parts. Ownership identifies who changes and accepts each part. A dependency determines what must be ready first. Parallel work helps when responsibilities are separable and contracts are clear.\",\n        \"example\": \"The API returns accessStatus; the UI must agree on its name and allowed values.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Two completed tasks automatically make one working feature.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Coordination cost and integration\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Coordination includes sharing context, resolving conflicts, reviewing assumptions and checking the assembled result. AI can manage this work too. Choose one agent or several according to the task, not to make the process look advanced.\",\n        \"example\": \"One agent is simpler for a small label change; separate UI and API work may fit a larger bounded feature.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Agent count is evidence of delivery skill.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Shared contract\",\n        \"runs\": \"Task / interface record\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Owned workstreams\",\n        \"runs\": \"One or several AI workers\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Integrated journey\",\n        \"runs\": \"Assembled product\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Change the field agreement between two prepared workstreams.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Both use accessStatus\",\n          \"result\": \"The parts can exchange the expected field; integration still needs checking.\",\n          \"why\": \"Agreement is necessary but not sufficient for a working feature.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"UI expects status\",\n          \"result\": \"The UI receives a different field and cannot reliably display access.\",\n          \"why\": \"Both parts can pass isolated checks while the contract is broken.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"One small task, one agent\",\n          \"result\": \"No cross-agent handoff needed; check the complete result directly.\",\n          \"why\": \"Avoid coordination work that adds no useful separation.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"Two agents implement different meanings of confirmed.\",\n      \"action\": \"Agree on the field, allowed values, ownership and a shared example before parallel work.\",\n      \"after\": \"Run the same example through the assembled UI and API.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"A coordinating agent can help, but a shared mistaken requirement can survive every agent's review.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"A change whose parts agree\",\n      \"brief\": \"Deliver a change touching the interface and service. Use one or multiple agents as useful, with an explicit contract and an end-to-end observation.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Draw which parts need work from other parts. Choose how people or agents will coordinate and explain why that approach fits.\",\n        \"Agree what the parts exchange using one successful message and one failure message. Give each worker a clearly owned part of the change.\",\n        \"Bring the parts together, investigate any mismatch, and repeat the complete user journey. Separate passing checks are not enough to show that the parts work together.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The interface contract names fields, allowed values and error meaning.\",\n        \"Ownership and sequencing are clear, including the decision about agent count.\",\n        \"A real integration observation covers both the normal path and a contract failure.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain a dependency, interface and coordination cost in your change.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find where each component produces or consumes the shared contract.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What if the API adds a new status the UI does not recognize?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare one worker with parallel workstreams for this task.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says all unit tests passing proves integration works. Identify a gap and a check.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Introduce a conflicting field or meaning and ask the learner to trace the consequence across parts.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"A response field is renamed in one component only. Diagnose the boundary.\",\n        \"The change becomes tiny after discovery. Reconsider parallel work.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [4, 8, 17, 28]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 18,\n    \"stage\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Add an AI product capability\",\n    \"problem\": \"Staff want help turning long visit notes into a short summary. Decide what information the model receives, what it may return and what happens when its output is wrong.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A justified AI feature with explicit uncertainty, contract and data boundaries.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"ai\", \"security\", \"evaluation\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Training, inference, tokens and context\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Training changes model parameters using data. Inference uses a trained model to produce output from supplied context. Tokens are units of representation and often billing. Context is the information available for this call; it is not automatically the complete business history.\",\n        \"example\": \"A summary call receives one authorized visit note and an output instruction.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A model automatically learns your latest records permanently from one call.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Uncertainty and structured output\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A plausible answer can be unsupported. A schema constrains output shape, not factual correctness. Validate the structure and evaluate whether meaning is supported by the input. Keep missing facts missing. Fine-tuning changes learned behaviour and differs from supplying current context or retrieval.\",\n        \"example\": \"A valid summary object can still invent an appointment date.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Valid JSON means the content is true.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Authorized note + instruction\",\n        \"runs\": \"Application server\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Inference\",\n        \"runs\": \"Configured model service or local runtime\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Validate + show summary\",\n        \"runs\": \"Application boundary\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Inspect a prepared output for a note that contains no appointment date. These are fixtures, not live model calls.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Summary with unknown date\",\n          \"result\": \"The shape is valid and the date remains unknown.\",\n          \"why\": \"This preserves a missing fact; broader quality still needs evaluation.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Valid JSON, invented date\",\n          \"result\": \"Shape passes; factual support fails.\",\n          \"why\": \"A schema cannot establish whether a statement is supported.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Malformed response\",\n          \"result\": \"Reject or recover without showing a misleading result.\",\n          \"why\": \"The integration must handle an invalid contract.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"A fluent summary adds a date not in the note.\",\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI to preserve missing information, validate output and evaluate supported facts on representative notes.\",\n      \"after\": \"The feature distinguishes output format from factual support and records remaining errors.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"If using fixtures, label them. A real model experiment requires explicit configuration and budget and a separately observed result.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"A note assistant with honest limits\",\n      \"brief\": \"Add a small note-summary feature with a replaceable model adapter, data boundary and supported-fact checks. A labelled fixture adapter is acceptable practice; it cannot establish real model quality.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Explain why this job needs AI rather than a simpler rule. Decide what information it may receive, what it should return and how it should handle missing facts.\",\n        \"Ask AI to build the connection and interface. Start with a clearly labelled fixed example, or configure an actual model with an agreed budget.\",\n        \"Try ordinary input, missing facts and wrongly shaped answers. Record the actual provider or runtime, settings, cost, or the limits of using fixed examples.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The actual inference or fixture path, context and data boundary are accurately identified.\",\n        \"Invalid structure and an unsupported factual claim are handled and recorded separately.\",\n        \"The feature has an observable benefit target and no unsupported claim of real-model reliability.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain training, inference, tokens, context and the difference between shape and truth.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find the actual model or fixture adapter and the validation boundary.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What happens when a note exceeds the available context or lacks a date?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare supplying context, retrieval and fine-tuning for changing visit information.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says a schema prevents hallucination. Demonstrate a well-shaped unsupported statement.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Use an unfamiliar note with a missing fact. Ask the learner to predict a safe result and explain evidence limits.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The provider's data policy no longer fits the customer. Reconsider the deployment.\",\n        \"The summary changes a tentative report into a certainty. Investigate the meaning error.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [15, 16, 17, 23]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 19,\n    \"stage\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Connect AI to knowledge and actions\",\n    \"problem\": \"The assistant needs current service policies and may propose changing a visit. Finding information and taking action require different contracts and authority.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A knowledge-assisted or bounded-action feature with source and permission evidence.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"ai\", \"agents\", \"security\", \"data\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Embeddings, retrieval and grounding\",\n        \"explanation\": \"An embedding represents data as numbers useful for comparing learned similarity. Retrieval selects candidate information; grounding connects an answer to evidence. Similarity does not prove relevance or truth. Sources can be outdated, contradictory or malicious.\",\n        \"example\": \"Retrieve the current cancellation policy and show the supporting passage and version.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A cited document is necessarily correct or actually supports the answer.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Tools, permissions and untrusted content\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A tool lets a model request a defined operation; the application validates arguments and authority before execution. Retrieved text is information, not permission. Limit access and use suitable approval or policy for consequential actions.\",\n        \"example\": \"A note saying ignore restrictions cannot authorize cancelling another customer's visit.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"The model choosing a tool proves the action is allowed.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Question + authorized context\",\n        \"runs\": \"Application\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Retrieve source / propose tool\",\n        \"runs\": \"Search + AI adapter\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Verify support / authority\",\n        \"runs\": \"Trusted application boundary\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Change the prepared evidence or tool request.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Current supporting policy\",\n          \"result\": \"Answer may cite the relevant passage while preserving its scope.\",\n          \"why\": \"Check the source, version and whether the claim follows from it.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Relevant but obsolete policy\",\n          \"result\": \"Do not present it as current policy; investigate the version.\",\n          \"why\": \"Similarity cannot establish freshness.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Document requests a cancellation\",\n          \"result\": \"Treat the instruction as untrusted content; enforce the user's actual authority.\",\n          \"why\": \"Information access is not action permission.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"The assistant executes a cancellation because a retrieved paragraph tells it to.\",\n      \"action\": \"Separate source material from instructions and enforce tool permissions outside the model.\",\n      \"after\": \"The answer may use an authorized policy; an unauthorized write is refused.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"A prepared retrieval example teaches the boundary. Test a real retriever separately before claiming search quality.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"An assistant with sources and boundaries\",\n      \"brief\": \"Connect the note assistant to a small versioned policy collection or a bounded visit tool. Explain the chosen retrieval or tool mechanism and test unsupported and unauthorized cases.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Choose which sources the assistant may read and which actions it may take. Write what it should say when information or permission is missing.\",\n        \"Ask AI to build a replaceable connection to those sources or actions. Record which source version was used and keep trusted checks separate from returned text.\",\n        \"Try a supported answer, a missing or outdated source, and a document that asks for an action the assistant is not allowed to take. Check the actual outcomes.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"A claim points to an inspected supporting passage or an action has an explicit authorized contract.\",\n        \"Missing or outdated evidence does not become an invented current answer.\",\n        \"Untrusted text cannot expand the feature's authority; the observed refusal is recorded.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain similarity, retrieval, grounding and tool authority using your feature.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find the source selection or tool-validation boundary in the actual system.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What if the nearest retrieved passage contradicts the current policy?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare keyword search, embeddings and direct structured queries for your information.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says a document's instruction authorizes its tool call. Explain the trust error and test the boundary.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Supply a plausible stale source or unauthorized request and ask for the safe result and supporting evidence.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"Two policy versions disagree. Explain how to resolve the answer.\",\n        \"A tool may read visits but is asked to cancel one. Trace the refusal.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [17, 18, 19, 20, 21]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 20,\n    \"stage\": 4,\n    \"title\": \"Compare and improve approaches\",\n    \"problem\": \"One impressive summary is not a measurement. Compare two approaches on the same representative cases, with quality, cost and delay visible.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A reproducible comparison with failures, uncertainty and a justified choice.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"evaluation\", \"evidence\", \"ai\", \"product\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Baselines, samples and held-out cases\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A baseline is a reference approach, possibly a simple rule or current process. Representative cases reflect real use. Held-out cases are kept out of tuning to probe transfer. Small or selected samples can give misleading estimates; report counts and uncertainty.\",\n        \"example\": \"Compare both assistants on the same notes, including missing and contradictory information.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Improving the examples used to tune a prompt proves improvement on new cases.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Base rates, precision, recall and cost\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Precision asks how many flagged cases really belong; recall asks how many relevant cases were found. Base rate is how common the condition is. A rare condition can yield many false alarms even with a seemingly accurate classifier. Measure latency and cost alongside errors.\",\n        \"example\": \"10 true alerts and 90 false alerts give 10% precision, not 90%.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"One accuracy percentage tells you every consequence of errors.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Fixed cases + expected outcomes\",\n        \"runs\": \"Versioned evaluation set\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Run both approaches\",\n        \"runs\": \"Actual adapters or labelled fixtures\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Errors + cost + latency\",\n        \"runs\": \"Comparison report\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Prepared alert results: 10 true positives, 90 false positives and 10 missed positives. Choose a metric.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Precision\",\n          \"result\": \"10 / (10 + 90) = 10%.\",\n          \"why\": \"Only one in ten raised alerts is a true positive.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Recall\",\n          \"result\": \"10 / (10 + 10) = 50%.\",\n          \"why\": \"Half the actual positives were found.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Claim reliable overall\",\n          \"result\": \"Unsupported without use-case thresholds, negative cases, sample quality and costs.\",\n          \"why\": \"A persuasive label cannot replace relevant evidence.\",\n          \"node\": 0\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"A new prompt succeeds on the five notes used to write it.\",\n      \"action\": \"Freeze expectations, add representative held-out cases and run both versions under the same conditions.\",\n      \"after\": \"Report counts by error type, measured delay and cost, including regressions.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"A small experiment supports a bounded observation, not a universal model ranking or future guarantee.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"A fair assistant comparison\",\n      \"brief\": \"Compare two implementations or configurations of the AI feature against a non-AI or existing-process baseline where meaningful. Keep tuning and held-out cases distinct.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Write the expected result for each test case before comparing approaches. Include ordinary cases, missing information and attempts to mislead the assistant. Decide which mistakes matter most.\",\n        \"Ask AI to run the same cases through each approach. Record the actual settings, usage and time taken so the comparison can be repeated.\",\n        \"Inspect failures and calculate the rates that matter to your users. Choose an approach and explain what the comparison still does not establish.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The comparison uses the same versioned cases and separates held-out cases from tuning.\",\n        \"The report includes counts, error types, latency and actual or clearly hypothetical cost.\",\n        \"The conclusion explains sample limitations, base rates and what evidence does not establish.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain baseline, held-out case, base rate, precision and recall with your results.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find raw observations supporting a number in the report.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What might happen to precision if the target condition becomes much rarer?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare a faster cheaper option with a better-scoring option using the cost of errors.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says a correlation between using the tool and fewer missed visits proves causation. What else could explain it?\",\n      \"guide\": \"Change the case distribution or error cost; check the learner can revise the interpretation and decision.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The evaluation excluded non-English notes but users need them. Reassess the claim.\",\n        \"The new approach has better recall and many more costly false alarms. Choose a threshold or workflow.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [15, 18, 22, 23]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 21,\n    \"stage\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"Ask AI to produce useful checks\",\n    \"problem\": \"AI has generated a green test suite, but a deliberately broken confirmation still passes. A check is useful when it can expose a relevant incorrect result.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A test strategy connected to requirements, including a demonstrated defect.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"quality\", \"evidence\", \"logic\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Unit, integration and end-to-end checks\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A unit check isolates a small rule. An integration check covers an agreement between parts. An end-to-end check follows a user journey across relevant components. These provide different evidence and different diagnosis costs.\",\n        \"example\": \"Check a quote rule, an API/database write, then a browser confirmation journey.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"More end-to-end checks automatically make all smaller checks unnecessary.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Regression and coverage limits\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A regression is previously required behaviour becoming incorrect after change. A regression check preserves that expectation. Coverage reports where execution went, not whether every important case and expectation was correct. Test data must represent relevant situations.\",\n        \"example\": \"The duplicate-submit check should fail when duplicate protection is deliberately removed in a test environment.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A green suite establishes requirements it never checked.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Required behaviour\",\n        \"runs\": \"Acceptance matrix\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Appropriate checks\",\n        \"runs\": \"Unit / integration / journey\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Defect exposed + fixed\",\n        \"runs\": \"Test environment\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"A deliberate defect writes the wrong visit but returns a success toast. Choose a check.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Assert toast only\",\n          \"result\": \"Passes while the relevant defect survives.\",\n          \"why\": \"The assertion misses record identity and stored state.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Assert selected record\",\n          \"result\": \"Fails when the wrong visit changes.\",\n          \"why\": \"The check observes the actual requirement.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Count test cases\",\n          \"result\": \"No conclusion about this defect.\",\n          \"why\": \"Quantity is not the same as a meaningful expectation.\",\n          \"node\": 0\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"Tests assert the same broken calculation used by the app.\",\n      \"action\": \"Write an expected example from the business rule and deliberately introduce a defect in a disposable test change.\",\n      \"after\": \"The check fails, AI fixes the cause, and the original expectation passes.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Do not weaken the expectation just to make the suite green. AI can generate and run every check; explain why it is relevant.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"Checks that can prove you wrong\",\n      \"brief\": \"Ask AI to create appropriate unit, integration and journey checks from the acceptance matrix. Demonstrate one real defect and keep its regression check.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Choose important requirements and write a check for each, including the result you expect to observe.\",\n        \"In a separate test environment, introduce or use a known mistake. Show that the relevant check fails because of that mistake.\",\n        \"Ask AI to investigate and fix the cause. Rerun the check and the affected user task, and record any remaining limits.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"Each important check has a requirement-derived expectation and suitable test data.\",\n        \"A deliberate or observed defect produces a meaningful failing check.\",\n        \"The fix passes the original expectation and relevant regression checks without hiding the failure.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain the different evidence from unit, integration and journey checks.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Show the expectation that exposed your defect and its source requirement.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What would still pass if the wrong visit changed but the toast looked correct?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare a faster isolated check with a broader journey check.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says all green means there are no bugs. Name an untested behaviour and how to investigate it.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Supply an uncovered edge case and ask the learner to choose evidence before running it.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The test uses only one customer, hiding an authorization bug. Improve the data.\",\n        \"A mocked provider passes while its real schema changed. Choose an additional boundary check.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [12, 22]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 22,\n    \"stage\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"Release with a way back\",\n    \"problem\": \"The app works locally but the release uses different configuration. Plan delivery and recovery together so a failed change does not become a guess under pressure.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A documented release and a demonstrated recovery in an appropriate environment.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"operations\", \"execution\", \"stack\", \"collaboration\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Version history, builds and environments\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Version history records changes and a known starting point. A build turns source and dependencies into runnable output. An environment supplies configuration, services and access. A working local setup may differ from the deployed one in any of these.\",\n        \"example\": \"A release points to a specific revision and dependency configuration.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"The latest files on a laptop identify exactly what users are running.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"CI/CD, rollback and state\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Continuous integration checks changes; delivery/deployment pipelines prepare or release them. Rollback restores a suitable previous application version. Stored data may need a separate restoration or compatible migration plan; reverting code does not undo every external action.\",\n        \"example\": \"An older release must still understand the current data schema before it is a safe rollback.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A green pipeline proves production is healthy.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Known revision + checks\",\n        \"runs\": \"Version control / CI\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Build + configure + release\",\n        \"runs\": \"Delivery environment\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Verify or recover\",\n        \"runs\": \"User journey + operations\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Change the release situation in this prepared model.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Build passes, secret absent\",\n          \"result\": \"Release can still fail at runtime configuration.\",\n          \"why\": \"Build success does not supply a missing server credential.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Code-only regression\",\n          \"result\": \"A compatible previous release may restore the journey.\",\n          \"why\": \"Verify the actual result after rollback.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Incompatible data migration\",\n          \"result\": \"Code rollback alone may be unsafe or insufficient.\",\n          \"why\": \"Stored-state compatibility needs its own recovery plan.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"Deploy whatever is in the current folder.\",\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI to release an identified revision with environment checks, smoke tests and a compatible recovery target.\",\n      \"after\": \"A failed test release can return to a known working journey.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Demonstrate recovery in a test environment first. Record exact commands or provider actions through AI-generated instructions you can explain.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"A release you can recover\",\n      \"brief\": \"Release the product to a suitable test or authorized public environment with identified source, configuration and a recovery procedure.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Ask AI to inspect the tools your project uses and write repeatable build and release instructions. List required setting names while keeping secret values private.\",\n        \"Release a version you can identify. Open that deployed version and verify its main user journey there.\",\n        \"In an appropriate test setting, introduce a failure you can reverse. Recover and check both the app's behaviour and the information it needs.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The running release is tied to an identified revision, build and environment.\",\n        \"An actual deployed user journey is observed separately from pipeline success.\",\n        \"A recovery demonstration restores required behaviour and accounts for stored-state compatibility.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain source, build, configuration, deployment and rollback in this release.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Identify the running version and where required configuration is supplied.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What might fail when local dependencies differ from the release environment?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare reverting code with restoring data and reconciling external effects.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says the green pipeline proves the site works for users. What observation is missing?\",\n      \"guide\": \"Change one environment variable or version in a test scenario and ask for a bounded investigation and recovery.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The previous code cannot read the new schema. Revise recovery.\",\n        \"The build succeeds but a server configuration value is missing. Trace the failure.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [8, 10, 14, 29, 30]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 23,\n    \"stage\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"Diagnose from evidence\",\n    \"problem\": \"Some confirmations are slow and others fail. Instead of asking AI to randomly rewrite the feature, collect evidence that distinguishes possible causes.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An incident investigation with a tested hypothesis and verified recovery.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"operations\", \"evidence\", \"architecture\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Logs, metrics and traces\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Logs record events, metrics summarize measurements, and traces connect steps of a request. Correlation identifiers help follow one action across components. Instrumentation should avoid secrets and unnecessary personal information.\",\n        \"example\": \"A request ID connects the browser error, API event and slow provider call.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"More raw logging automatically makes diagnosis easier or safer.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Hypotheses and falsification\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A hypothesis is a possible explanation with a predicted observation. Choose evidence that can distinguish it from another explanation. AI can investigate and propose fixes; verify the actual user flow after change rather than treating a convincing diagnosis as the result.\",\n        \"example\": \"If the provider is slow, its span should dominate latency while local validation remains fast.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"The first plausible explanation is the established cause.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"User symptom + request ID\",\n        \"runs\": \"Browser / support report\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Compare hypotheses\",\n        \"runs\": \"Logs + metrics + traces\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Fix + verify journey\",\n        \"runs\": \"Test and running service\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Prepared trace: total 2,000 ms; validation 20 ms; database 80 ms; provider 1,900 ms. Choose an investigation.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Rewrite CSS\",\n          \"result\": \"No evidence links styling to the measured server delay.\",\n          \"why\": \"Choose a hypothesis connected to the observed boundary.\",\n          \"node\": 0\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Inspect provider span\",\n          \"result\": \"A relevant next step: check its timing, errors and comparison requests.\",\n          \"why\": \"The trace identifies a candidate; it does not yet explain why the provider is slow.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Declare cause solved\",\n          \"result\": \"Premature: no corrective change or recovered journey observed.\",\n          \"why\": \"Diagnosis and verified recovery are different outcomes.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"A user reports slow saves; AI suggests a database rewrite.\",\n      \"action\": \"Collect a correlated trace and compare a provider-delay hypothesis with a database-delay hypothesis.\",\n      \"after\": \"Evidence directs a bounded change, followed by a timed user journey.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Record contradictory evidence and remaining uncertainty. Do not expose private notes in diagnostic artifacts.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"An evidence-led incident record\",\n      \"brief\": \"Instrument and investigate one real or deliberately injected test failure using AI, with privacy-aware observations and a verified fix.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Write what went wrong, where it happened and two possible causes.\",\n        \"Ask AI to gather records, measurements or request traces that could distinguish those causes. Remove private information from anything you share.\",\n        \"Make a small fix and repeat the affected task. Record the cause, supporting observations, recovery result and anything that remains uncertain.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The incident record separates symptom, hypothesis and observed evidence.\",\n        \"A correlated observation supports or refutes a hypothesis without leaking credentials or private data.\",\n        \"The relevant user journey works after the fix and its observed result is recorded.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain a log, metric, trace and hypothesis through this incident.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find the observation that changed the investigation's direction.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What would you expect to see if the database were the bottleneck instead?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare collecting all payloads with targeted privacy-aware instrumentation.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says the last changed file must be the cause. Propose a distinguishing investigation.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Provide contradictory evidence and see whether the learner revises the explanation rather than defending the first guess.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"Only one region experiences failures. Refine the hypotheses.\",\n        \"Logs contain a credential. Explain the immediate handling and instrumentation change.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [6, 25]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 24,\n    \"stage\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"Manage growing use and cost\",\n    \"problem\": \"The system feels slow as appointments grow. Caching, indexing and queues solve different problems and introduce tradeoffs. Measure before choosing.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A measured bottleneck improvement with cost and correctness limits.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"architecture\", \"data\", \"evaluation\", \"product\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Latency, throughput and algorithmic cost\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Latency is time per operation; throughput is completed work per time. A data structure organizes information and affects available operations. Algorithmic cost describes how work grows with input size. An index can speed a query while adding storage and write cost.\",\n        \"example\": \"Scanning every visit grows differently from a suitable indexed lookup.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A faster one-user response proves the system handles more simultaneous users.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Caching, queues and consistency\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A cache reuses a result and can become stale. A queue defers work and needs failure and retry rules. Consistency concerns when different observations agree. Choose freshness and delay according to the business task, not just a benchmark.\",\n        \"example\": \"A cached policy may tolerate a refresh delay; a last-slot reservation needs a protected current decision.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Caching every response preserves correct behaviour automatically.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Representative workload\",\n        \"runs\": \"Controlled measurement\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Chosen intervention\",\n        \"runs\": \"Query / cache / queue boundary\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Measure result + tradeoff\",\n        \"runs\": \"Quality and cost report\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Change the prepared design for repeated policy reads.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Always fetch current\",\n          \"result\": \"Freshness follows the source; each read pays the source latency and cost.\",\n          \"why\": \"This favours current information over reuse.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Cache for 10 minutes\",\n          \"result\": \"Repeated reads may be faster but can show a policy up to the freshness limit out of date.\",\n          \"why\": \"Invalidation and an accepted freshness policy are part of the design.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Queue the read\",\n          \"result\": \"Work is deferred; this alone does not make an interactive answer faster.\",\n          \"why\": \"Queues fit suitable background tasks, not every delay.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"A broad scan gets slower as the visit table grows.\",\n      \"action\": \"Measure representative queries, ask AI to propose an index, and compare the same workload afterwards.\",\n      \"after\": \"Report read improvement, write/storage effects and limitations of the test load.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Synthetic measurements establish behaviour under the stated test conditions, not unlimited production capacity.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"One measured performance improvement\",\n      \"brief\": \"Identify one actual bottleneck in the growing workflow, compare an appropriate improvement and report correctness, cost and measurement limits.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Choose work that resembles real use. Decide how to measure waiting time and work completed, and state what must remain correct.\",\n        \"Ask AI to measure the current system and suggest one suitable change. Options might include faster data lookup, a saved copy called a cache, or a queue for work that can wait.\",\n        \"Repeat the same workload and user task. Record any improvement and resource cost, plus effects on old data or repeated requests.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"Before and after measurements use comparable data, workload and environment.\",\n        \"The change improves the chosen measure or is honestly rejected from the results.\",\n        \"Correctness, freshness or retry behaviour remains acceptable and added cost is recorded.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain latency, throughput, workload growth and the selected mechanism.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find the measured bottleneck and actual boundary changed.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What changes as record count or concurrent demand increases?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare two plausible interventions with cost and consistency tradeoffs.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says a 10-minute cache always gives current availability. Show the counterexample and a suitable policy.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Change load or freshness needs; ask the learner to revisit the design rather than recite a speed figure.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The cached data now determines the last available slot. Reconsider correctness.\",\n        \"The queue receives work faster than it can finish. Predict backlog and recovery needs.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [5, 18, 23, 24, 25]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 25,\n    \"stage\": 5,\n    \"title\": \"Change the system without losing trust\",\n    \"problem\": \"A new visit field and a dependency update are needed. The running system contains useful records, permissions and deletion commitments that must survive the change.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A rehearsed migration or dependency change with restoration evidence.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"data\", \"operations\", \"security\", \"stack\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Migrations, backups and restoration\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A migration changes stored structure or data. A backup is a recoverable copy under a defined scope. Restoration is the act of rebuilding usable state from that copy. A backup is not proven usable until a relevant restore has been checked.\",\n        \"example\": \"Add an access-status field without silently converting unknown values into confirmed.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Reverting source code automatically reverses a data migration.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Compatibility, privacy and retention\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Dependency versions and data formats define compatibility. Privacy includes what is collected, who can access it, how long it is retained and how deletion reaches relevant copies. A restore plan must consider permissions and data commitments, not just row counts.\",\n        \"example\": \"A restored copy should not re-enable a deleted user's access.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A backup can be kept forever without any data responsibility.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Known state + recoverable copy\",\n        \"runs\": \"Test data / backup scope\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Rehearsed change\",\n        \"runs\": \"Migration or dependency update\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Verify + restore if needed\",\n        \"runs\": \"Application + data + permissions\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Choose the prepared recovery evidence.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Backup file exists\",\n          \"result\": \"A copy exists; usability is unverified.\",\n          \"why\": \"The file may be incomplete, inaccessible or incompatible.\",\n          \"node\": 0\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Restored and queried\",\n          \"result\": \"Relevant state can be read; check permissions and required journeys too.\",\n          \"why\": \"Restoration evidence should match what the product depends on.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Only code reverted\",\n          \"result\": \"Stored data and external effects may remain changed.\",\n          \"why\": \"Application history and data history are different boundaries.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"A migration maps every blank access value to confirmed.\",\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI to rehearse on a copy, preserve unknown meaning, compare records and test restoration.\",\n      \"after\": \"The new version preserves information meaning and has an observed recovery path.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Protect fictional test copies appropriately too. Never treat a production data change as disposable practice.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"A change with a recovery rehearsal\",\n      \"brief\": \"Use AI to perform a data migration or dependency update in a test copy. Verify required data, permissions and behaviour, and demonstrate restoration.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Before changing the system, record which versions must work together, what the data means, how long it must be kept and what you need to recover.\",\n        \"Create a suitable test copy. Ask AI to rehearse the change and run checks of the behaviour and information that must be preserved.\",\n        \"Restore from the recovery copy and check the main user task. Record what this practice recovery showed and what has not yet been tested.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"Important record counts, relationships and meanings are compared before and after.\",\n        \"Required permissions and privacy/retention decisions survive or have an explicit migration policy.\",\n        \"A restoration is actually demonstrated and the required user journey is verified afterwards.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain migration, backup, restoration and compatibility in your change.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find the actual stored format, dependency version and recovery copy.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What if an older app reads the newly migrated data?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare rollback, forward correction and restoring a backup for this situation.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says a backup's filename proves recoverability. What demonstration is needed?\",\n      \"guide\": \"Ask about a missing record, revoked permission or incompatible reader after recovery; check the explanation concerns the actual system.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"A restored copy contains a previously deleted account. Revise recovery handling.\",\n        \"The new dependency changes a default used by the API. Investigate compatibility.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [6, 8, 10, 24, 29]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 26,\n    \"stage\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"Start an independent domain project\",\n    \"problem\": \"Transfer the concepts to an unfamiliar workflow: building maintenance, a clinic's non-clinical administration, logistics, education or another authorized domain. A familiar demo is not evidence that you understand this setting.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A domain-grounded project brief with a discovered requirement and measurement plan.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"product\", \"evidence\", \"collaboration\", \"security\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Domain constraints and incentives\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Domain knowledge explains the real work and its limits. Incentives explain why people may adopt, avoid or work around a change. The person requesting the app may not be the person doing the extra work. Investigate both perspectives.\",\n        \"example\": \"A manager wants more completed visits; technicians need travel and access time counted fairly.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A reusable app template establishes the requirements of every business.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Success agreement and uncertainty\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A brief records the problem, source evidence, scope, authority, constraints and success conditions. Uncertainty belongs in the brief as questions and assumptions. AI can research and prepare it, but invented details must stay labelled.\",\n        \"example\": \"Separate an observed missed handoff from a guess about its annual cost.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A detailed AI-written proposal makes an unverified workflow factual.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Unfamiliar workflow\",\n        \"runs\": \"Domain participants / authorized sources\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Evidence + missing requirement\",\n        \"runs\": \"Investigation\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Agreed or labelled brief\",\n        \"runs\": \"Project decision record\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Choose what you discover during a maintenance-workflow investigation.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Manager wants faster dispatch\",\n          \"result\": \"A goal to examine with the people doing the work.\",\n          \"why\": \"Speed may conflict with equipment, travel or access constraints.\",\n          \"node\": 0\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Technician lacks access approval\",\n          \"result\": \"A missing requirement that changes the workflow and acceptance criteria.\",\n          \"why\": \"A new scheduling screen alone would not resolve the blockage.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"AI estimates savings\",\n          \"result\": \"A hypothesis requiring assumptions and measurements.\",\n          \"why\": \"Generated numbers are not observed business benefit.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"Reuse the appointment app unchanged in a building-maintenance company.\",\n      \"action\": \"Investigate a recent visit and discover site-access approval and equipment qualifications.\",\n      \"after\": \"The brief changes the allowed assignments and definition of a preventable missed visit.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"The concepts transfer; the domain assumptions require fresh evidence.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"An independent outcome brief\",\n      \"brief\": \"Investigate an unfamiliar authorized domain and define a useful improvement with participants or a clearly labelled scenario. Include local language, connectivity, cost and data constraints.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Choose a real-world setting, what you have permission to access and where you can gather evidence. Use AI to help research and prepare questions.\",\n        \"Draw the current workflow. Explain why people would use or avoid a change, and find one requirement missing from the first request.\",\n        \"Compare buying a tool, configuring one, connecting services and building something. Agree the useful result, scope and measurement plan, or clearly label practice assumptions.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The brief identifies sources, users, incentives and at least one discovered requirement.\",\n        \"Success, scope, authority and constraints are explicit without invented measurement.\",\n        \"An alternative approach and a plan to measure actual benefit are documented.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain what domain understanding changed in your initial idea.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find the source behind the discovered requirement and an important unknown.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What if the person entering data gains extra work but no benefit?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare the selected approach with buying or configuring an existing product.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says an old demo proves this workflow's business case. What fresh evidence is needed?\",\n      \"guide\": \"Introduce a plausible domain constraint omitted from the brief and ask the learner to revise scope and success.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The workflow must support low connectivity and two languages. Revise the brief.\",\n        \"The measured delay includes customer cancellations. Reassess the outcome definition.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [26, 28]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 27,\n    \"stage\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"Deliver through AI\",\n    \"problem\": \"Deliver the independent brief with the AI available to you. A polished artifact must be accompanied by an accurate explanation of its actual stack, information flow and decisions.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A working solution and a maintained system explanation tied to real observations.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"agents\", \"stack\", \"architecture\", \"quality\", \"collaboration\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Architecture as responsibilities and tradeoffs\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Architecture describes responsibilities, boundaries and dependencies. Coupling is how strongly one part depends on another. Modularity can make change easier, but extra services also add operations and failure boundaries. Choose complexity proportionate to the outcome.\",\n        \"example\": \"One application may fit a small workflow better than several separately deployed services.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"More layers always mean more professional architecture.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Evidence-linked delivery\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Delivery combines implementation, checks, configuration, access and a usable result. AI may perform the whole technical sequence. Keep the system explanation synchronized with meaningful changes and distinguish actual observations from planned checks.\",\n        \"example\": \"A claimed enquiry feature needs evidence that the enquiry reaches the chosen destination.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A generated system description is accurate because the app looks complete.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Brief + acceptance + boundaries\",\n        \"runs\": \"Independent project\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"AI-led implementation + checks\",\n        \"runs\": \"Actual chosen stack\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Working outcome + explanation\",\n        \"runs\": \"Demonstration evidence\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Change the prepared architecture for a small information-only service.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Static site fits the need\",\n          \"result\": \"Explain files, browser behaviour and hosting. No invented backend is needed.\",\n          \"why\": \"The explanation must match the actual product's responsibilities.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Needs shared private records\",\n          \"result\": \"Add suitable storage, identity and trusted permission boundaries.\",\n          \"why\": \"The requirement adds responsibilities beyond a static page.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Add many services anyway\",\n          \"result\": \"More configuration and failure boundaries without an established benefit.\",\n          \"why\": \"Complexity needs a reason connected to constraints.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"The diagram shows a database and model service copied from a template.\",\n      \"action\": \"Inspect the actual project with AI and trace a real action from start to result.\",\n      \"after\": \"The diagram contains only implemented components, with planned extensions labelled.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"A static solution can be correct. A backend solution needs deeper data, access and failure evidence as those responsibilities appear.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"The independent working solution\",\n      \"brief\": \"Use capable AI to deliver the mission-26 brief. Choose suitable technologies, implement the useful workflow and maintain an accurate explanation throughout.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Give AI the evidence, expected-result table, allowed actions and budget for your independent project. Ask for a plan that fits those limits.\",\n        \"Build in small useful pieces. Inspect the technologies actually used and keep all eight areas of your system explanation up to date.\",\n        \"Demonstrate the main user task, a relevant failure, and the rules protecting data and access. Record what works and what still needs attention.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The actual product demonstrates the brief's primary useful outcome.\",\n        \"Checks cover relevant normal, failure and access/data cases with reproducible observations.\",\n        \"The system explanation matches the real files, dependencies, runtime, hosting and information flow.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain the architecture and how its boundaries serve the outcome.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Identify every important technology in actual files, configuration or service settings.\",\n      \"predict\": \"Choose one failed component and predict what the user and stored state would show.\",\n      \"compare\": \"Defend an important technology choice against a simpler or more portable alternative.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI's diagram includes an unused service. Explain how to verify and correct the description.\",\n      \"guide\": \"Choose an action from the live product, trace its real path and ask a new requirement question with AI available.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The budget is cut while the outcome stays the same. Revise architecture or scope.\",\n        \"A key provider becomes unavailable. Explain a proportionate alternative.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [14, 21, 24, 28, 29]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 28,\n    \"stage\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"Respond to reality\",\n    \"problem\": \"The product works technically, but staff avoid it. Learn whether it improves the whole workflow, causes extra work elsewhere or misses a practical constraint.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An observed feedback cycle, a changed requirement and a bounded benefit claim.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"product\", \"evidence\", \"collaboration\", \"operations\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Adoption, training and downstream effects\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Adoption means people can and choose to use the result in their work. Training, trust, time and incentives affect it. A faster step can move a bottleneck or increase another person's workload. Observe the whole journey, not just the new screen.\",\n        \"example\": \"Dispatch saves time while technicians spend longer correcting incomplete access details.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A successful deployment proves useful adoption.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Feedback, maintenance and causal limits\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Feedback is evidence about experience and outcomes, with a source and context. Maintenance includes correcting defects and adapting to changed conditions. Compare outcomes carefully: selection, seasonality and other changes can explain a before/after difference.\",\n        \"example\": \"Report a small pilot's observed rate without claiming it proves the app caused every improvement.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Positive comments alone establish financial benefit.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Actual or labelled pilot use\",\n        \"runs\": \"People + workflow\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Feedback + outcome measures\",\n        \"runs\": \"Evidence record\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Adapt + recheck\",\n        \"runs\": \"Product + whole workflow\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"The prepared pilot reduces dispatch time by 10 minutes per day. Change what happens downstream.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"No extra downstream work\",\n          \"result\": \"A candidate improvement, subject to measurement and sample limits.\",\n          \"why\": \"Still check data quality and other changes before a causal claim.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"20 extra minutes correcting visits\",\n          \"result\": \"The local speedup may worsen total workflow effort.\",\n          \"why\": \"Evaluate the whole outcome rather than the favoured step.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Staff do not use it\",\n          \"result\": \"Investigate access, fit, training and incentives before claiming impact.\",\n          \"why\": \"Technical availability and adoption differ.\",\n          \"node\": 0\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"The dashboard is live, but staff keep a separate spreadsheet.\",\n      \"action\": \"Observe the task, discover missing offline access and compare total workflow effort.\",\n      \"after\": \"Adapt the product and training, then repeat a bounded pilot.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Clearly label scenario feedback when real participants are unavailable. Do not invent customers or benefit measurements.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"An improvement tested in use\",\n      \"brief\": \"Run an authorized pilot or clearly labelled scenario trial of the independent solution. Investigate adoption and a changed requirement, then improve and remeasure.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Plan a small trial with people who agree to take part. Decide what result to measure and which outcomes must not get worse.\",\n        \"Observe use. Record a specific confusion, extra work created by the change and a requirement or limit that changed.\",\n        \"Ask AI for a suitable improvement. Update your system explanation, then repeat the relevant task and measurement to check the effect.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"Feedback and outcome observations have sources and an honest real/scenario label.\",\n        \"A changed requirement leads to a justified product or process improvement.\",\n        \"The benefit statement includes downstream effects, uncertainty and remaining adoption gaps.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain the difference between deployment, adoption and demonstrated benefit.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Show evidence for a changed requirement and its resulting decision.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What if the faster workflow transfers work to another person?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare a training change with a software change for the observed confusion.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says happier pilot users prove the product caused a 20% business improvement. What evidence is missing?\",\n      \"guide\": \"Offer contradictory pilot feedback or a different denominator and ask the learner to revise the conclusion.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The most active users are also the most experienced staff. Reassess the comparison.\",\n        \"The product meets its target but creates extra work for customers. Revise the guardrail.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [7, 26, 29, 30]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 29,\n    \"stage\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"Transfer the work and adapt\",\n    \"problem\": \"Another person must operate the product without relying on your memory. The current AI tool or provider may also become unsuitable. Preserve the useful outcome while changing how it is delivered.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"A usable handoff and a demonstrated transfer to an unfamiliar tool or changed constraint.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"collaboration\", \"operations\", \"stack\", \"product\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Handoff, ownership and runbooks\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A handoff transfers the knowledge, access and responsibility needed to operate the result. A runbook describes observable symptoms and appropriate actions. Name ownership, escalation and maintenance decisions. Documentation must match the current product and avoid embedding secrets.\",\n        \"example\": \"A new operator can start the service, verify a visit and follow recovery instructions.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A long README proves another person can run the product.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Portability and conceptual transfer\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Portability concerns moving data and behaviour across tools, services or environments. Conceptual transfer means applying a mental model in an unfamiliar setting. An adapter can isolate provider-specific details, but formats, policy and operational differences still need checks.\",\n        \"example\": \"Replace an AI provider while preserving the output contract and rerunning the same evaluation.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Changing a model name guarantees equivalent behaviour and cost.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Current product + explanation\",\n        \"runs\": \"Known stack\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"New operator or tool\",\n        \"runs\": \"Fresh environment / replacement\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Same required outcome\",\n        \"runs\": \"Rechecked journey + evidence\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Choose what the prepared handoff includes.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Source files only\",\n          \"result\": \"Setup, configuration, ownership and recovery may remain unknown.\",\n          \"why\": \"Implementation alone does not transfer operational knowledge.\",\n          \"node\": 0\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Guide + fresh operator trial\",\n          \"result\": \"Observed confusion can reveal missing instructions and assumptions.\",\n          \"why\": \"The handoff is evaluated by someone completing the task.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Provider swapped, no checks\",\n          \"result\": \"Compatibility and quality remain unverified.\",\n          \"why\": \"A stable interface does not guarantee equivalent semantics or economics.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"Only the original builder knows the hidden configuration.\",\n      \"action\": \"Ask AI to draft a current setup/runbook and have another person or a fresh-context assistant follow it, labelling which was used.\",\n      \"after\": \"Fix missing steps and rerun the journey with a replacement component or unfamiliar environment.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"A fresh-context AI trial is useful evidence about instructions; it is not evidence that a human operator adopted the product.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"A handoff that survives change\",\n      \"brief\": \"Transfer the independent solution to another operator and adapt one tool, provider or environment while preserving required outcomes.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Prepare a current system drawing and instructions for setup, setting names, costs, ownership, data export and recovery.\",\n        \"Ask a new operator to try the instructions and record where they get stuck. If this is a simulated fresh-start trial, label it that way. Improve the instructions from the observations.\",\n        \"Replace one meaningful part with an unfamiliar tool. Check that its messages and results still fit the other parts, then repeat quality checks and the relevant user journey.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The handoff trial reaches the useful outcome from documented starting conditions.\",\n        \"Ownership, configuration, cost, data handling and recovery instructions match the actual product without secrets.\",\n        \"A replacement or unfamiliar tool preserves required behaviour with observed checks and documented differences.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain the operational knowledge transferred and the concept reused across tools.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Find the provider-specific boundary and an actual instruction corrected by the trial.\",\n      \"predict\": \"What might change in quality, latency or permissions after replacement?\",\n      \"compare\": \"Compare an adapter change with a full rebuild for your portability need.\",\n      \"challenge\": \"AI says identical schemas make two providers interchangeable. Which behaviours and costs need comparison?\",\n      \"guide\": \"Ask the learner to explain an unfamiliar tool through the same concepts and demonstrate a relevant handoff task.\",\n      \"variations\": [\n        \"The operator cannot access the original AI account. Revise the handoff.\",\n        \"The replacement service has a different data-retention policy. Reassess suitability.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"reuse\": [10, 25, 27, 29, 30]\n  },\n  {\n    \"id\": 30,\n    \"stage\": 6,\n    \"title\": \"Demonstrate professional capability\",\n    \"problem\": \"Present useful work and explain it under a fresh condition. The final challenge tests concepts and delivery with AI available, not memorized syntax or a polished presentation alone.\",\n    \"outcome\": \"An evidence-based capability review, an adapted product and an honest next-practice plan.\",\n    \"foundation\": [\"collaboration\", \"evidence\", \"product\", \"agents\", \"quality\", \"stack\"],\n    \"concepts\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Demonstration and fresh-scenario reasoning\",\n        \"explanation\": \"A demonstration connects a claim to an observable result. A fresh scenario changes a requirement or failure condition to test whether a mental model transfers. AI remains available; the learner must apply the concepts, make predictions and explain evidence rather than repeat a prepared script.\",\n        \"example\": \"Explain the real stack, then adapt when a provider is unavailable or a client changes the goal.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"Course completion automatically equals years of professional employment.\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Capability, uncertainty and adaptation\",\n        \"explanation\": \"Capability is demonstrated responsibility under stated conditions. Record help, decisions, time, costs, gaps and review. Future opportunities depend on technology, adoption and institutions. Planning and coordination may also be automated; keep learning and compare outcomes with comparable tools.\",\n        \"example\": \"A reviewer records which concepts transferred and which need another demonstration.\",\n        \"misconception\": \"A new job title guarantees a permanently protected career.\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"flow\": [\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Useful product + evidence\",\n        \"runs\": \"Independent project\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Fresh question + changed condition\",\n        \"runs\": \"Learner + available AI + reviewer\"\n      },\n      {\n        \"name\": \"Observed capability + gaps\",\n        \"runs\": \"Review and next-practice record\"\n      }\n    ],\n    \"experiment\": {\n      \"prompt\": \"Choose the proposed evidence for graduation.\",\n      \"options\": [\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Polished demo alone\",\n          \"result\": \"Product appearance is visible; conceptual transfer is unestablished.\",\n          \"why\": \"Ask the learner to explain actual technologies and respond to a fresh change.\",\n          \"node\": 0\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Working product + generated script\",\n          \"result\": \"The output may be good while understanding remains untested.\",\n          \"why\": \"Follow-up predictions and investigations must establish the learner's applied reasoning.\",\n          \"node\": 1\n        },\n        {\n          \"label\": \"Outcome + explanation + fresh change\",\n          \"result\": \"Relevant capability evidence under the stated tools and review conditions.\",\n          \"why\": \"Record limits honestly; it is not an employment guarantee or authenticated credential.\",\n          \"node\": 2\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"example\": {\n      \"before\": \"A learner presents an impressive system but cannot say where its records live.\",\n      \"action\": \"Revisit storage with an experiment, then ask for a new prediction and trace in their actual product.\",\n      \"after\": \"Record demonstrated reasoning or a specific gap needing further practice.\",\n      \"lesson\": \"Useful AI output and learner understanding are separate standards. Manual coding is not required to meet either standard.\"\n    },\n    \"project\": {\n      \"title\": \"The independent delivery challenge\",\n      \"brief\": \"Investigate an unfamiliar business problem, use available AI to improve it, explain the actual technologies and concepts, demonstrate benefit, and adapt when conditions change.\",\n      \"steps\": [\n        \"Gather your independent product, eight-part system explanation, observations, costs and feedback. State what you and AI each contributed.\",\n        \"Ask a reviewer to choose a new requirement or failure that fits your product. Predict what it affects, then use AI to adapt the system and verify the result.\",\n        \"Review the shared foundations, record what you demonstrated and what needs practice, and choose the next task. Any comparison with experienced people needs actual comparable tasks, tools and observations.\"\n      ],\n      \"checks\": [\n        \"The demonstrated product addresses an evidenced or explicitly labelled scenario problem with proportionate outcome checks.\",\n        \"The learner explains the actual stack, runtime, action flow, data, authority, choices and failure behaviour.\",\n        \"A fresh condition is investigated and handled with AI available; the review records observed reasoning, limits and next practice.\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"assess\": {\n      \"explain\": \"Explain the purpose, system and important decisions without relying on a prepared script.\",\n      \"locate\": \"Trace one reviewer-chosen action through the actual technologies and evidence